    "Mixture asymmetry is much weaker than reported: the SAME 1-parameter constrained mixture (model = w*other1 + (1-w)*other2, no intercept; 2 parameters incl. the implicit mean) explains C at residual fraction 0.2814 (R2 0.9208), B at 0.4533 (R2 0.7945) and A at 0.4242 (R2 0.8201). The report compares C's 1-parameter mixture (28.1% residual) against the 2-predictor+intercept fits for B (40.9%) and A (42.4%), which use one more free parameter, and then concludes the redundancy is 'one-directional'. On the like-for-like constrained mixture the three models are nearly equally reproducible (C 28.1% vs B ~29.6% vs A ~33.0%), so the 'C carries little beyond the mixture but A and B retain larger unique components' claim is overstated.",
    "Sample-level correlations (r_AB=0.773, r_AC=0.900, r_BC=0.907 over 1698 samples) are labelled 'descriptive only' in the JSON and no p-values were derived from them; the whole-pass-level rms correlations (0.939/0.973/0.985, n=22) are also descriptive. No pass-level inference is reported for these r's, so no blocked-test violation here.",
    "wilcoxon_p = 4.76837158203125e-07 for B/A and C/A is the exact enumeration FLOOR for n=22 (2/2^21): the report quotes it to 3 significant figures as if it were a resolved estimate (self-report says 'p <= 4.6e-06'). Ordering conclusions do not depend on the digits, but the figure 'p = 4.77e-07' should be read as 'p <= 4.77e-07'.",
    "High-leverage pass: pass_01 alone has per-pass RMS ~0.118/0.115/0.116 mm/s for A/B/C, ~6-9x the median pass. Its raw ratios are B/A=0.9736, C/A=0.9800, B/C=0.9935 (all near 1), so including it drags the median B/C ratio UP (0.817 vs 0.8132 without pass_01). The ordering still holds in every pass, so the conclusion is robust, but any pooled/aggregate statistic is dominated by this one pass - confirmed by the item-5d pooled ratios (B/A 0.755 pooled vs 0.541 median-of-passes).",
    "Constructed (orthogonalised) residuals are exact: no centring inconsistency detected.",
    "Bootstrap CIs are 95% marginal percentile intervals for the population MEDIAN ratio, one pair at a time. The report's headline ('all 22/22 passes rank B<A, C<A, B<C') is a sign-test-like statement whose p-value is not the quoted Wilcoxon p; under the null of exchangeability the chance of 22/22 is 2*(0.5^22) = 4.77e-07. This supports, rather than undermines, the reported ordering.",
